KNOWN BUGS
==========

KB-1  character-select pins: hit point offset from the visible pin
--------------------------------------------------------------
Where:  VS mode and every menu using the red/blue player chips
        (character select, mode select).
Seen:   to drop a pin on a portrait, the cursor must sit slightly
        down/left of where the pin appears to point; effective click
        area sometimes feels further out than the drawn pin.

Status: UNCORRECTED, cause identified in the original 2006 code.
        Do not "fix" in the emulator; see options below.

Cause (from recovered sources, frame_318/PlaceObject2_251_player1_chip_*):

    on(press):    this.startDrag(true, 0, 0, 700, 400);
    on(release):  this.stopDrag(); detect();
    detect():     if (this.center.hitTest(_parent.blade)) { ...select... }

Two 2006 design decisions combine:

1. startDrag lockCenter=true -- while dragging, the mouse holds the
   chip's registration point, not the visible pin tip. If the pin
   graphic is drawn offset from its registration point (it is; the
   ball slider in the same screen uses a y=-6.5 offset line, same
   authoring habit), the cursor is never "on" the drawn tip.

2. The drop test is `this.center.hitTest(portrait)` -- only a child
   clip named `center` counts, not the whole pin. Its position inside
   the chip symbol determines where a portrait actually registers.
   Wherever `center` sits relative to the drawn tip IS the click
   offset you feel.

So the offset is faithful original behavior, not a Ruffle bug. It
would have behaved the same on Flash Player 8.

LibreWolf note: privacy.resistFingerprinting=true rounds pointer
coordinates (~2px) and clamps devicePixelRatio. It cannot create a
large consistent offset, but it can make edge cases feel worse.
Cross-check: same page at zoom 100% in a stock Firefox/Chromium --
if the offset persists identically (expected), it is KB-1 as
described; if it changes with zoom, that is a separate Ruffle
scaling bug worth reporting upstream.

Fix options, in risk order:
  a) accept it (it is authentic 2006 behavior; keyboard select works
     fine and was the primary path anyway);
  b) SWF surgery: open symbol 251/254 in FFDec, move the `center`
     child onto the visible pin tip, save. This is a real fix but it
     edits a binary the whole bundle embeds -- every consumer of
     SuperSmash.swf changes, and FFDec round-trips carry corruption
     risk. Deliberately NOT done here; the author said fix only what
     you are sure of, and "sure of the cause" is not "sure of the
     edit". Revisit with a diff-test: patched vs original bundle,
     both played to character select, before shipping anything.

